<section class="section reveal" id="random-generation">
  <span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span>
  <h2>Random Number Generation and Distributions</h2>

  <p>Random number generation is fundamental to machine learning. You need it for initializing neural network weights, creating synthetic data, implementing stochastic algorithms, and validating models with randomized tests. Understanding how to generate random numbers from different distributions is essential.</p>

  <div class="analogy">
    <h4>💡 Think of It This Way</h4>
    <p>Imagine you have a spinner labeled with numbers. The probability of landing on each number follows a distribution. NumPy's random functions simulate spinning different types of spinners—uniform (all numbers equally likely), normal (most spins near the center), etc.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Basic Random Number Generation</h3>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Random number generation</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="im">np</span>

<span class="cm"># Set seed for reproducibility (ALWAYS do this in production!)</span>
<span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">seed</span>(<span class="nm">42</span>)

<span class="cm"># Uniform distribution [0, 1)</span>
<span class="va">uniform</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>(<span class="nm">5</span>)       <span class="cm"># [0.37..., 0.95..., ...]</span>

<span class="cm"># Normal (Gaussian) distribution</span>
<span class="va">normal</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">randn</span>(<span class="nm">1000</span>)   <span class="cm"># mean=0, std=1</span>

<span class="cm"># Integer random values [0, 10)</span>
<span class="va">integers</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">randint</span>(<span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="nm">5</span>)  <span class="cm"># [3, 7, 2, ...]</span>

<span class="cm"># Shuffling</span>
<span class="va">arr</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">arange</span>(<span class="nm">10</span>)
<span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">shuffle</span>(<span class="va">arr</span>)  <span class="cm"># In-place shuffle</span>

<span class="cm"># Sampling without replacement</span>
<span class="va">sample</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">choice</span>(<span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">arange</span>(<span class="nm">10</span>), <span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="va">replace</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">False</span>)</code></pre>

  <h3>Probability Distributions</h3>

  <p>Different distributions model different phenomena. Choose the right distribution for your data generation or hypothesis testing.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Common probability distributions</span>

<span class="cm"># Exponential (waiting times, lifetimes)</span>
<span class="va">exponential</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">exponential</span>(<span class="va">scale</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">2.0</span>, <span class="va">size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1000</span>)

<span class="cm"># Poisson (counts of events)</span>
<span class="va">poisson</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">poisson</span>(<span class="va">lam</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="va">size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1000</span>)

<span class="cm"># Binomial (successes in n trials)</span>
<span class="va">binomial</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">binomial</span>(<span class="va">n</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="va">p</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.5</span>, <span class="va">size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1000</span>)

<span class="cm"># Beta (proportions, probabilities)</span>
<span class="va">beta</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">beta</span>(<span class="va">a</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="va">b</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="va">size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1000</span>)</code></pre>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Always set a seed for reproducibility in production code</li>
      <li>Normal distribution (randn) is most common for continuous data</li>
      <li>Uniform (random) for unbiased sampling</li>
      <li>Match distribution to your problem domain and data generation process</li>
      <li>Use randint for discrete values, choice for categorical sampling</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

