<section class="section reveal" id="statistical-functions">
  <span class="section-label">Core Functions</span>
  <h2>Descriptive Statistics and Analysis</h2>

  <p>NumPy provides efficient functions for computing statistics across arrays. The axis parameter is crucial—it determines whether you compute statistics across all data or within specific dimensions.</p>

  <h3>Computing Summary Statistics</h3>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Descriptive statistics</span>
<span class="va">data</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>], [<span class="nm">4</span>, <span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="nm">6</span>]])  <span class="cm"># Shape: (2, 3)</span>

<span class="cm"># Global statistics (across all elements)</span>
<span class="va">mean_all</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">mean</span>(<span class="va">data</span>)           <span class="cm"># 3.5</span>
<span class="va">std_all</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">std</span>(<span class="va">data</span>)            <span class="cm"># 1.707...</span>

<span class="cm"># Per-column statistics (axis=0 reduces rows)</span>
<span class="va">mean_cols</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">mean</span>(<span class="va">data</span>, <span class="va">axis</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0</span>)    <span class="cm"># [2.5, 3.5, 4.5]</span>

<span class="cm"># Per-row statistics (axis=1 reduces columns)</span>
<span class="va">mean_rows</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">mean</span>(<span class="va">data</span>, <span class="va">axis</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1</span>)    <span class="cm"># [2, 5]</span>

<span class="cm"># Other statistics</span>
<span class="va">min_val</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">min</span>(<span class="va">data</span>)              <span class="cm"># 1</span>
<span class="va">max_val</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">max</span>(<span class="va">data</span>)              <span class="cm"># 6</span>
<span class="va">median</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">median</span>(<span class="va">data</span>)          <span class="cm"># 3.5</span>
<span class="va">var</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">var</span>(<span class="va">data</span>)                <span class="cm"># 2.916... (std^2)</span>
<span class="va">quantile</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">percentile</span>(<span class="va">data</span>, <span class="nm">75</span>) <span class="cm"># 75th percentile</span></code></pre>

  <h3>Correlation and Covariance</h3>

  <p>Understand relationships between variables. Correlation measures linear association; covariance measures joint variability.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Correlation and covariance</span>
<span class="va">x</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">randn</span>(<span class="nm">100</span>)
<span class="va">y</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">2</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="va">x</span> <span class="op">+</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">randn</span>(<span class="nm">100</span>) <span class="cm"># y strongly related to x</span>

<span class="cm"># Pearson correlation coefficient</span>
<span class="va">correlation</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">corrcoef</span>(<span class="va">x</span>, <span class="va">y</span>)[<span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>]  <span class="cm"># ~0.89 (strong positive)</span>

<span class="cm"># Covariance matrix</span>
<span class="va">cov_matrix</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">cov</span>(<span class="va">x</span>, <span class="va">y</span>)
<span class="cm"># [[var(x), cov(x,y)],</span>
<span class="cm">#  [cov(x,y), var(y)]]</span>

<span class="cm"># Multi-variate correlation</span>
<span class="va">data_matrix</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">randn</span>(<span class="nm">100</span>, <span class="nm">5</span>)  <span class="cm"># 100 samples, 5 features</span>
<span class="va">correlation_matrix</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">corrcoef</span>(<span class="va">data_matrix</span>.<span class="fn">T</span>)  <span class="cm"># (5, 5) correlation matrix</span></code></pre>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>🌍 Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In Exploratory Data Analysis:</strong> Computing statistics quickly reveals data patterns. Missing values, outliers, and skewness become visible through summary statistics.</p>
    <p><strong>In Feature Engineering:</strong> Correlation reveals which variables are redundant. High correlation suggests potential multicollinearity in regression.</p>
    <p><strong>In Hypothesis Testing:</strong> Statistics enable statistical inference and validation of assumptions underlying machine learning models.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Use axis parameter to control dimensionality of statistical results</li>
      <li>Correlation and covariance reveal variable relationships</li>
      <li>Median is robust to outliers; use for skewed data</li>
      <li>Standard deviation measures spread; variance is std^2</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

